Single-heade myosin II acts as a dominant-negative mutation in
Dictyostelium.

C. Geoffrey Burns, Denis A. Larochelle, Harold Erickson, Mary Reedy 
and Arturo De Lozanne

Department of Cell Biology, Duke University Medical Center
Durham, NC 27710

Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U.S.A., in press.

Summary:

   Conventional myosin II is an essential protein for cytokinesis,
capping of cell surface receptors and development of Dictyostelium
cells.  Myosin II also plays an important role in the polarization and
movement of cells.  All conventional myosins are double-headed
molecules but the significance of this structure is not understood
since single-headed myosin II can produce movement and force in vitro.
We found that expression of the tail portion of myosin II in
Dictyostelium led to the formation of single-headed myosin II in vivo.
The resultant cells contain an approximately equal ratio of double-
and single-headed myosin II molecules.  Surprisingly, these cells were
completely blocked in cytokinesis and capping of concanavalin A
receptors although development into fruiting bodies was not impaired.
We found that this phenotype is not due to defects in myosin light
chain phosphorylation.  These results show that single-headed myosin
II cannot function properly in vivo and that it acts as a
dominant-negative mutation for myosin II function.  These results
suggest the possibility that cooperativity of myosin II heads is
critical for force production in vivo.

